; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   126   *  Os_TrapIsTrapForWriteAccessRequired()
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   127   **********************************************************************************************************************/
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   128  /*! \brief          Returns whether caller of an API has to trap for write access.
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   129   *  \details        Performing a trap is typically very time consuming. For this reason the OS tries to prevent it.
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   130   *                  If the current thread is already privileged, the OS does not have to trap. On some platforms this
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   131   *                  optimization is not possible, because the hardware does not allow to efficiently check whether the
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   132   *                  current thread is privileged. In this case, UserMode is assumed as soon as MemoryProtection is
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   133   *                  enabled.
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   134   *
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   135   *  \retval         !0  Trap is required, caller thread does not have write privileges.
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   136   *  \retval         0   Trap is not required, caller thread has write privileges.
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   137   *
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   138   *  \context        OS_INTERNAL
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   139   *
